## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of ordered porous materials formed by self-assembly of organic ligand molecules and metal ion clusters through coordination bonds. The unique frame structure of MOFs has a large specific surface area and the participation of metal ion clusters provides a good number of active sites. In the past few decades, MOFs have developed rapidly in many fields such as catalysis[1], sensing[2], drug transport[3], gas separation[1,4] and energy storage 5]. The use of pyridine derivatives as the nodes of the MOFs material allows a large number of photophysical electrons to conduct from the pyridine ring to the center of the metal Ru, thereby greatly improving the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) performance[6]. Ln-MOF synthesized by Hao et al. shows high selectivity and sensitivity to small organic molecules and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions[7]. Peng et al. designed an adjustable pore size Ni-MOF as an ssDNA transport vector and ssDNA was encapsulated by the MOFs to avoid nuclease breakdown[8]. Hu et al. mixed Cu-MOF as a base film with polyimide to selectively adsorb and separate different gases [9]. Xu et al. developed a simple “heat-assisted space-limited powdering” method to prepare sub- $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ ultra-small MOFs nanocrystals with high-efficiency electrochemical energy storage[10]. In addition, the unique frame structure of MOFs has many advantages including large specific surface area, adjustable pore size, versatile framework structure and easy surface modification. It can be used as a precursor to modify the surface of metal oxides for improving the catalytic properties of materials[11]. In order to enhance the conductivity of MOFs materials, many scholars have combined MOFs materials with graphene oxide or carbon nanotubes with excellent conductivity to obtain MOFs based materials with excellent conductivity and porosity[12,13].
Environmental degradation has become a major problem that needs to be solved urgently. Dry batteries are necessary for humans, but usually used batteries contain heavy metals such as cadmium and mercury[14]. The ability to pollute the environment is unpredictable. One button battery can contaminate 600,000 liters of water. Today, the use of rechargeable batteries greatly reduces the use of dry batteries and reduces the pollution of heavy metal ion. New electrode materials such as graphene oxide [15], conductive polymer[16], metal oxide[17]/hydroxide[18] and new metal organic framework materials [19, 20] have been slowly walking into people's lives. MOFs with abundant pore structure have many underpotential deposition sites, which can be modified by conductive polymers, metal oxides, graphene oxide and other materials to improve the electrochemical performance of the MOFs. In addition, the adjustable advantages of MOF in terms of structure and functionality make it a highly potential energy storage material. Electrochemical deposition combining polyaniline with Co-MOF can produce a flexible solid-state supercapacitor with excellent electrical conductivity[21]. Zn-doped Ni-MOF shows large specific capacitance, outstanding capacitance retention rate and good cycle stability[22]. The high-temperature carbonized MOF derivatives have a variety of morphologies that enrich their use in supercapacitors[23].
In this paper, MOFs were used as precursors to prepare excellent supercapacitor materials through non-metallic elements comosition. The non-metallic elements (NME) were modified on the surface of the synthesized three-dimensional metal organic framework material. Electrochemical performance test indicated the composited MOFs materials had excellent electrochemical performance. This tentative result effectively improved the electrochemical performance of MOFs parent, and the resulted composition material could be used as a capacitor material without pollution to provide good storage performance in social life in the future.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
All reagents in the experiment are of analytical grade and used as received. 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid (BTC, $98.0\%$ ) was purchased from Beijing HWRK Co., Ltd, and the 4,4'-bipyridyl (TPAL, $98.0\%$ ) was purchased from Aladdin. Sodium dihydrogen phosphate $(99\%)$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(98.0\%)$ were purchased from Tianjin Zhiyuan Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Both urea $(99.5~\%)$ ) and thioacetamide (TAA, 99.0 $\%$ ) were bought from Beijing Chaoyang Chemical. N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, 99.5 $\%$ ) was obtained from Covalent. Distilled deionized water was used throughout.
## 2.2 Characterizations
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained through a BRUKER X-ray diffractometer (Germany), using $\mathrm{{Cu-K(a)}}$ radiation. Physical adsorption measurements were carried out using a Micromeritics ASAP 2010 sorptometer (USA) in a liquid nitrogen atmosphere at ${}^{-196}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ThermoFisher Scientific, ESCALAB 250Xi, USA) was used to measure the chemical composition and chemical state of the elements on the surface of the material. Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) of the samples were achieved on a FTS-3000 spectrometer (Finland) in the range of $4000{\sim}\overbrace{400\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}}$ in the form of KBr tablets. Thermal stability was evaluated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Perkin-Elmer, SSC-5200, USA) from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and energy dispersive spectrum (EDS) were measured on a Zeiss Ultra Plus field emission scanning electron microscope (Germany). Cyclic voltammetry (CV), Galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) and impedance were tested using a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation (Chenhua, Shanghai, China). Cyclic stability testing was performed on a CT2001A-LAND cell test system (Landian, Wuhan, China).
## 2.3 Synthesis of Ni-MOF
The precursor MOFs material was prepared by a conventional hydrothermal method previously reported in the literature[24]. The typical procedure was as follows: $0.7012{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of BTC and $\ensuremath{0.7815\mathrm{~g~}}$ of TPAL were dissolved in $60~\mathrm{mL}$ solution with $40~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol and $20~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. $\ensuremath{1.4542\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solution of ethanol and DMF of the same ratio. Two clear solutions were mixed and transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, and then the sealed autoclave was transferred in an oven and maintained at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days. The product was collected by filtration and washed with the mother liquor and deionized water 3 times, and dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to get the Ni-MOFs materials.
## 2.4 Preparation of the composite materials
Scheme 1 is a synthetic flow diagram in which $600~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of Ni-MOF dispersed in distilled water and then heated to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with stirring. The salt solution containing 14.0 mM urea was dropped into the aforementioned solution and was continually stirred at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, it was transferred into a lined polyfluortetraethylene reactor and reacted at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The product was collected, washed with distilled water, and dried in a vacuum oven at $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ to get the resulting composite N@Ni-MOF. P-modified $({\mathrm{P}}{\ @}{\mathrm{Ni-MOF}})$ and S-modified (S@Ni-MOF) were obtained by the same method respectively using sodium dihydrogen phosphate and thioacetamide instead of urea. It is worth noting that the details need to be paid attention to during the synthesis of $\mathbf{S}@\mathbf{Ni-MOF}$ materials. A small amount of sodium hydroxide needed to be added to the reaction system to hydrolyze thioacetamide to $S^{2\cdot}$ , which prevented the hydrolysis of thioacetamide into SO42- in aqueous solution.
