## 2.5 Electrochemical measurement
The electrochemical properties of Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF nanomaterials were characterized with dripping method[25] using a three-electrode system at $3\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH. In the three-electrode system, the working electrode was prepared by dispersing $8~\mathrm{mg}$ of the above materials and $1.4~\mathrm{mg}$ of acetylene black in $400{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of 0.5 $\%$ nafion solution. Then $6~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the resultant homogeneous slurry was dropped onto the pretreated glassy carbon electrode (GCE) and dried at room temperature. The counter electrode (CE) and reference electrode (RE) are platinum electrode and saturated calomel electrode respectively. The assembly method of the all-solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor was reported in the literature[26], and the specific process is shown in the Scheme $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the active materials and acetylene black (according to the ratio of active substance: acetylene black: binder 80:15:5) were weighed, and a drop of the already diluted binder was added dropwise. The mixture was ultrasonically dispersed in a $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol solution for 30 minutes, and then baked to a viscous state under an infrared lamp and pressed into a sheet, and then the sheet was uniformly spread on a surface of $1\mathrm{cm}\times2$ cm of foamed nickel, and the effective area was $1\mathrm{cm}\times1$ cm. The synthesis method of the PVA/KOH conductive gel was obtained according to the reference[27].
Scheme 2 A schematic diagram of assembling the all-solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor.
The specific capacitance of single electrode in the three-electrode configuration is calculated from GCD curves[28].
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\pmb{C}=\frac{I\Delta t}{m\Delta V}}\end{array}
$$
In this equation, $\pmb{I}$ refers to the discharge current (A), $\Delta t$ represents the discharge time (s), ${\pmb m}({\pmb m}={\pmb m}_{a}+{\pmb m}_{c}$ in the all-solid-state asymmetric system) is the mass of the active material $(\mathbf{g})$ , and $\Delta V$ is the range of GCD voltage (V).
Assemble an all-solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) to estimate actual application value. Materials such as $\mathbf{S}@$ Ni-MOF were used as anode materials and activated carbon was used as a cathode material. To ensure that the anode and cathode charges are equal, the mass ratio of the anode and cathode materials was calculated according to the following equation[29].
$$
\pmb{R}=\frac{\pmb{m}_{+}}{\pmb{m}_{-}}=\frac{\pmb{C}_{-}\cdot\Delta\pmb{V}_{-}}{\pmb{C}_{+}\cdot\Delta\pmb{V}_{+}}
$$
Where $\mathbf{\delta}_{m}$ is the mass loading, $c$ is the specific capacitance, and $\Delta V$ is the voltage range for anode $(+)$ and cathode $(-)$ electrodes, respectively.
Energy density $(\mathrm{E},\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\cdot$ ) and power density $(\mathbf{P},\mathbf{W}\mathbf{kg}^{-1})$ are calculated based on equations (3) and (4) [30]:
$\begin{array}{l}{E=\frac{C\Delta V^{2}}{2}}\\ {P=\frac{E}{\Delta t}}\end{array}$ (3) (4)
Where $c$ is the specific capacitance of the ASC calculated according to the GCD curves, $\Delta t(\mathrm s)$ and $\Delta V$ represent discharge time and voltage range $\left(\Delta V=V_{-}\ +\ V_{+}\right)$ , respectively.
3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterizations
The spatial mechanism diagram of Materials Studio fitting Ni-MOF material is shown in Figure 1a (Space group $\mathrm{P}{\cdot}62\mathrm{m}$ , $\mathbf{a}=\mathbf{b}=16.634\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , $\mathrm{c}=11.234\mathrm{\AA}$ , ${\mathfrak{a}}=\upbeta=90^{\circ}$ ， $\gamma=120^{\circ})$ . Through the constructed crystal model, the fitted PXRD spectrum was consistent with the peak position of the actual test results, and the strong diffraction peaks of Ni-MOF at $2\theta=7.8^{\circ}$ , $10.6^{\circ}$ , $15.7^{\circ}$ , $18.4^{\circ}$ and $23.7^{\circ}$ were attributable to the (011), (110), (002), (300) and (003) planes, respectively. It was found that the fitted XRD and the actually measured data corresponded uniformly at the peak position, but there was difference in intensity, which was attributed to crystal defects. XPS results also confirmed this conclusion. Figure 1b shows the PXRD of the modified Ni-MOF, and a, b, c curves correspond N@Ni-MOF, P@Ni-MOF and $\mathbf{S}\ @$ Ni-MOF respectively. By comparison with standard PDF cards, it was found that non-metals were enriched in the precursor Ni-MOF to form corresponding composites. The non-metals N, P and S enter the vacancies to form the corresponding $\mathrm{Ni_{x}N_{y}}$ , $\mathrm{Ni_{x}P_{y}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{x}S_{y}}$ , which compensates for the defects of the crystal structure, increases the UPD active sites, and improves the electrochemical performance. By non-metal modification of Ni-MOF, the crystal defects are effectively compensated and the stability of the crystal structure was improved (consistent with the thermogravimetric results below). The XPS analysis of the precursor Ni-MOF with corrected binding energy based on $\mathrm{C_{ls}}=284.6\:\mathrm{eV}$ showed that the synthesized MOF material had four elements of C, N, $\mathrm{~o~}$ and Ni, which further demonstrated the successful synthesis of the material (Figure 1c). $\mathrm{Ni}_{2\mathrm{p}}$ peak further confirmed the existence of Ni in the structure and also opened up the possibility of electrochemical redox. The curves of nickel can be fitted to two spin-orbit doublets as shown in inset of Figure 1c: two peaks exhibited at 852.8 and $870.4{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~eV}}}$ can be assigned to $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ , and the other two peaks centered at 874.8 and $851.9\mathrm{eV}$ should be attributed to the $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . The peak positions of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ reported in the literature [31] are 854.9, 876 eV and 856.6，873.3 eV, respectively. Due to the entry of impurity atoms into the gap position, the lattice is deformed, which brings additional energy boost. Therefore, the binding energy of each peak is slightly different from that reported in the literature. In addition, XPS spectrum of S@Ni-MOF exhibited a peak of S2p around $168.8\ \mathrm{eV}.$ After post-treatment, four peaks at 162.1, 163.3, 168.8 and $169.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ were observed and attributed to $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{S}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S}^{[32,33]}$ , NiS and ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-}$ , respectively. At the same time, it also proves the successful composition of S and Ni-MOF and formation of S-S bonds (Figure 1d). The nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms of the Ni-MOF (Figure 1e) displayed a type IV curve with a BET specific surface area of $758.24~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The isotherm showed a monolayer and multi-layer adsorption at a low relative pressure $(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}<0.70)$ with an obvious hysteresis loop in the relative high pressure $\mathrm{(P/P_{0}\geq}$
0.70), which unique frame structure offered a possibility for high electrochemical performance. The pore size distribution curve in the inset showed that the pore size distribution was concentrated to 13.46 Å, which was a microspore type. The isotherms of S-modified Ni-MOF did not change significantly, which meant that S atoms did not affect the framework structure of Ni-MOF. S atoms entered the vacancy of the crystal defect and occupied a certain space, so that the specific surface area $(537.47\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and the average pore size (10.58Å) were reduced. The fact that S atoms occupied the coordination hole effectively improved the charge transfer between the electrode surface and the electrolyte through reducing the internal resistance of the material itself.
The morphology and microstructure of Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF materials were observed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) and transmission electron micrograph (TEM). Figure 2a shows that the Ni-MOF precursor is a rod-shaped material assembled by strip nanosheets. Urea treatment peeled off the rod precursor material to obtain nanosheets by comparison with Ni-MOF（(Figure 2b). As can be seen from Figure 2c, an uniform parallelogram structure was obtained after treatment with sodium dihydrogen phosphate. Thus, sodium dihydrogen phosphate cleaved the precursor MOFs uniformly. Ni-MOF modified by thioacetamide was a needle-like deposit. Due to the coordination of the solvent with the central metal atom in the synthesis process, after drying, the solvent volatilized to obtain the defect crystal. After modification, the S atom coordinated with the vacant Ni, and the S coordination of different metal centers formed S-S with each other, which consisted with the XPS results in the previous section. Strong covalent bonds changed the topography of the raw materials (Figure 2d). The EDS surface sweep profiles of Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF show that the elements in the synthesis process and the modified elements are uniformly distributed in the material (Figure S2), indicating that Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF successfully synthesized. The morphology of Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF composites were further investigated by TEM. It can be seen that the corresponding topographical structures are completely similar. Figure 2e shows the rod-like structure and the layers are stacked. Figure 2f shows the irregular structure of the fragments. The parallelogram structure of Figure $\pmb{2\upmu}$ is regular but the thickness is large and opaque. Figure 2h shows a graphene-like pleated structure due to S-S agglomeration, but the presence of lattice is more clearly seen in this figure. The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images of Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF show more structural diversity and lattice distribution (Figure S3).
