## 3.2 Electrochemical characterization
The electrochemical performances of the nanocomposites were analyzed using CV and GCD measurements in 3 M KOH aqueous solution. Figure 3a shows the CV curve of Ni-MOF and N, P, S modified precursors at a scan rate of $30\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ . All curves in the figure exhibit a pair of redox peaks, indicating rapid reversible Faradaic redox reactions on the surface of the electrodes. It can be seen that the redox peak position of different non-metal modified precursors shifts to both ends and the potential window increases at the same scan rate. The modified Ni-MOF materials have larger potential windows, which may be because the ions enter the crystal defect vacancies, forming more active sites of underpotential deposition (UPD). The specific capacitance is determined according to the magnitude of the redox peak current. The GCD curves of the four materials are all house-shaped, further indicating that the materials are the electrode materials of pseudocapacitance behavior (Figure 3b). In addition, the three modified materials have a better discharge time than the precursor at a current density of $1\mathrm{\Ag^{-1}}$ . The relationship between the specific capacitance and the current density of the four materials is shown in Figure 3c. The modified P@Ni-MOF and S@Ni-MOF materials effectively improve the energy storage behavior of the precursor, but the $\mathbf{N}$ modification does not improve. Since urea destroys the three-dimensional structure of the precursor, the active site of the UPD decreases. In addition, the Nyquist plots of the four materials show straight lines in low-frequency regions and the semi-circle in the high-frequency region, corresponding to the electron transfer resistance inside the materials and the electronic interface transfer limit, respectively (Figure 3d). Obviously, the modified materials have smaller internal resistances, and the slopes of the high frequency region also confirm the more excellent conductivity of the modified materials in interface mass transfer[35]. In addition, according to the fitting equivalent circuit, it can be known that the composite material S@Ni-MOF has a Cdl component, indicating that the material has an ideal capacitive behavior. Among them, Rs, Rct, Rf, Cdl and Q represent internal impedance, resistance between electrode and electrolyte, formation of sheet resistance, double layer capacitor and constant phase angle component, respectively. From the fitting circuit data, the Rct value after S modification was $10.62\Omega$ .
Figure 3 Electrochemical performances of Ni-MOF and NME $@$ Ni-MOF electrodes measured in 3.0 M KOH solution. (a) CV curves of four electrode materials measured at $30\mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ . (b) GCD curves of four electrode materials measured at $\mathbf{1A}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . (c) The curves of specific capacitance versus current density for four different materials. (d) Nyquist plots of the precursor and three composite electrode materials in 3M KOH in the frequency range of $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ (The inset is the equivalent circuit of S@Ni-MOF).
Figure 4a shows the CV curves at a scan rate of $5\sim100\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ for ${\bf S}@{\bf N i-M O F},$ clearly showing the presence of a pair of redox peaks. As the scan rate increases, the peak potential also moves to both ends, and the peak shape does not change significantly, indicating that the material has good reversibility. Figure 4b shows the GCD curves of $\mathbf{S}@\mathbf{Ni-MOF}$ at different current densities and the specific capacitance can reach $1,453.5\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}~}$ . When the current density increases to $2,5,8,10,20\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacitances are 1,297.50, 1,111.46, 1,008.84, 953.75, 774.17 F g-1 , respectively. It is well known that the decrease of capacitance with the increase of current density is mainly due to the low utilization rate of the active material at a large charge and discharge current density[36]. Compared with other composite materials reported recently, the synthesized ${\bf S}@{\bf N i-M O F}$ has the biggest specific capacitance (Table 1). The peak current is linear with $\upnu^{1/2}$ , indicating that $\mathbf{S}@\mathbf{Ni-MOF}$ is a diffusion-controlled electrode material (Figure 4c). After 5,000 cycles of GCD experiments, the retention rate is over $89.23\ \%$ , and the decrease in capacitance retention is attributed to the long-term immersion of the electrode sheets, which cause the active material to fall off (Figure 4d). Through the above electrochemical tests, the S-modified MOFs precursor has good electrochemical properties and can be used as an electrode material in actual production.
## 3.3 S@Ni-MOF//AC Asymmetric Supercapacitors
To evaluate the practical energy density, an asymmetric supercapacitor device was assembled by using $\mathbf{S}_{}^{\mathcal{(a)}}$ Ni-MOF and AC respectively as positive and negative electrodes. As shown in Figure 5a, obviously, CV curve of AC has a potential window of $-1.0\sim0\mathrm{~V~}$ , and CV curve of the S@Ni-MOF electrode shows a redox peaks as a consequence of the Faraday pseudocapacitance behavior feature in the range from 0 to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ . So the minimum operation cell voltage of the $\upmu$ Ni-MOF//AC device can be extended up to $1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ . To further confirm the voltage window of the asymmetric supercapacitor, we choose the same scan rate and change the voltage window. When the voltage window is increased to $1.8~\mathrm{V},$ polarization occurs at the anode, which proves that the voltage window of $0\sim1.6{\mathrm{~V~}}$ we selected is suitable (Figure 5b). In addition, Figure 5b and 5c show that the assembled asymmetric $\mathbf{S}@$ Ni-MOF//AC supercapacitors are dominated by our synthetic $\mathbf{S}\textcircled{a}$ Ni-MOF. According to the GCD curves (Figure 5d), the specific capacitance of $136.5\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the energy density of $48.54~\mathrm{Wh\kg^{-1}}$ and the power density of $800\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ at the current density of $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ are calculated using the formula (1), (3) and (4). The energy density of $56.86~\mathrm{Wh\kg^{-1}}$ and the power density of $480~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ are measured at a current density of $0.6\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . Our work showed higher energy density and power density than that reported by previous studies (Figure 5e). After 20,000 cycles of testing, the capacitance is maintained at $86.67\%$ (Figure 5f). After 2,000 cycles of testing, the amount of capacitance retention decreased, which may be attributed to the deactivation of the electrode sheet surface active material. In the range of $10{,}000\sim$ 12,000 cycles, the amount of capacitance retention increases (up to $108.89\%$ ), and due to long-term recycling, the OH- of electrolyte gel enters the inside of the electrode sheet and activates the unreacted $\mathbf{S}\ @$ -Ni-MOF. It is then attenuated again because the active substance is inactivated and eventually stabilize (the retention rate was $89.23\%$ ). Comparing the shape of the first 10 cycles of the figure with that of the last 10 cycles, the behavior of the pseudocapacitance dominated by $\mathbf{S}@\mathbf{Ni-MOF}$ is weakened and gradually tends to the behavior of the electric double layer, largely because of the active substance inactivated.
