## 4. Conclusion
To conclude, we designed and prepared S@Ni-MOF nanocomposite with high-performance supercapacitor behavior by a two-step hydrothermal growth method. For the first time, non-metal was placed in the crystal defects of Ni-MOF to enhance the stability of the Ni-MOF structure and the unique frame structure. The non-metal modified Ni-MOF compensates for the crystal defects and increases the underpotential deposition sites, which is more conducive to the Faraday reaction. The ${\bf S}@{\bf N i-M O F}$ showed the highest specific capacitance of $1,453.5\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}~}$ and stable cycle performance with $89.23\ \%$ capacitance retention after 5,000 cycles. The assembled all-solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor exhibited a high energy density of $56.85~\mathrm{Wh~kg^{-1}}$ at power density of $480~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ , which could illuminate commercial red LED lamps. And after 20, 000 cycles, the capacitance remained at $86.67\%$ . These excellent electrochemical properties may be attributed to the formation of a more stable framework structure and more active sites after S modification. The prepared materials could be used in real-life portable electronic mobile devices and the developed preparation method also provide a general strategy for improve electrochemical performance of MOF based materials.
